Title: Blog about the opinion: Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp.

# One Scope to Rule Them All: Federal Circuit Demands Consistency Across DuPont Factors

## Why this case matters

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is an important reminder that the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board cannot define the relevant marketplace or class of consumers for one DuPont factor and then change the rules for another without explanation. In a short, pragmatic opinion, the Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding that Apex’s and CC Serve’s services were highly similar (the second DuPont factor), but vacated the Board’s treatment of the sixth DuPont factor — and, as a consequence, the first factor — because the Board improperly narrowed the universe of third‑party “Aspire” uses it would consider. The court’s instruction is straightforward: once the Board fixes the scope of similarity for goods and services, it must carry that scope through its evaluation of other DuPont factors that rely on the same underlying marketplace concept.

That instruction matters in practice because many DuPont inquiries depend on how the Board characterizes the relevant consumers and the relatedness of the parties’ goods or services. A defendant (or applicant) who wins a narrow characterization at step two should not be permitted to escape a crowded‑field analysis at step six by artful narrowing; nor should an applicant be able to stretch one finding across factors without record support. Apex clarifies the correct mechanics: similarity under factor two informs which third‑party marks are relevant under factor six, and that link must be respected.

## What the court did and why

The Board found that CC Serve’s ASPIRE registration (for credit card services) and Apex’s proposed ASPIRE BANK mark (for banking and financing services) were highly similar — even “legally identical, in part” — and therefore treated the second DuPont factor as weighing heavily in favor of confusion. But when the Board turned to the sixth factor (the number and nature of similar marks in use on similar goods), it limited its inquiry to third‑party uses of Aspire in the credit‑card context and excluded Aspire uses in other banking and financing contexts. The Federal Circuit held that bifurcating the analytical scope in that way was erroneous: the sixth factor only requires similarity, not identity, of goods and services, and where the Board has already found high similarity (or partial legal identity), it must use that same breadth when evaluating the sixth factor.

The court relied on settled precedent — Olde Tyme Foods, Juice Generation, and related decisions — which long ago made clear that the sixth factor looks to similar, not necessarily identical, goods and services. Because the Board’s factor‑two finding encompassed banking and financing as sufficiently like credit‑card services, excluding third‑party uses tied to banking or other financing services from the factor‑six inquiry was inconsistent with that earlier finding. The court therefore vacated the Board’s factor‑six finding and remanded for reconsideration. The court also vacated and remanded the first DuPont factor (similarity of the marks) because the commercial impression analysis can shift depending on how crowded or weak a mark is found to be under factor six.

## Analytical perspective: coherence, not uniformity

The Federal Circuit’s holding is doctrinally modest but practically significant. Modest, because the court did not announce a new test or rework DuPont; it applied existing precedents about relatedness and crowded fields. Significant, because it enforces a coherence principle that the Board must already have been following in theory but sometimes neglected in practice: the factual findings that drive one DuPont factor (particularly the characterization of the relevant marketplace and consumers) should inform the scope of evidence considered under other factors.

This coherence principle does not mean that the Board is mechanically bound to replicate every finding across all factors. DuPont is flexible: the Board may, with reasoned explanation and evidentiary support, adopt narrower or broader characterizations for particular inquiries (for example, focusing on a particular subset of consumers when evaluating actual confusion). But Apex underscores that any such narrowing must be justified on the record. The Board cannot, after finding the services “highly similar,” suddenly constrict the third‑party mark universe to a subset that produces a different result without reconciling that choice with its prior findings.

The court’s vacatur of factor one is also analytically sound. The similarity‑of‑marks analysis is not performed in isolation; the mark’s commercial strength — which the sixth factor informs — affects whether consumers are likely to confuse two marks that look or sound alike. Thus a remand on factor six can and often will change the factual backdrop for factor one.

## Doctrinal implications and downstream effects

Practically, Apex will change how counsel litigate oppositions and dispositions before the TTAB in several predictable ways. First, litigants will be more likely to submit broad third‑party evidence across all related banking and financial services once a Board finding narrows or broadens the relevant marketplace. Applicants should prepare to defend a wide universe of third‑party uses if the Board has already found goods or services similar. Conversely, registrants who secure a finding of similarity for one factor should be prepared to defend the same scope when the opponent offers crowded‑field evidence.

Second, the opinion invites the Board to be clearer and more explicit in its findings about the relevant consumer class and why it chooses any narrower scope for later DuPont inquiries. The Board can still focus on the most relevant subset of consumers, but it must tie that narrowing to record evidence (e.g., distinct channels of trade, separate target consumers, or limiting nature of the goods) rather than invoke different scopes for different factors as a matter of convenience.

Third, expect more remands in cases where the Board’s DuPont analysis shows internal inconsistencies between findings. That will likely increase the evidentiary burden on both sides to assemble comprehensive third‑party use proofs early, and to develop argumentation about why a narrower or broader universe of marks is appropriate for particular DuPont inquiries.

Finally, Apex reinforces a salutary limit on cherry‑picking. Trademark adjudication turns on rival considerations: mark similarity, goods’ relatedness, marketplace reality, and the overlay of third‑party use. The Federal Circuit’s direction here nudges the Board toward a more holistic, internally consistent analysis that better tracks consumers’ real‑world decisionmaking.

## Bottom line

Apex Bank v. CC Serve is a useful corrective: it tells the TTAB (and litigants) to be consistent in how they define the relevant marketplace across DuPont factors. Similarity in one factor should generally mean similar scope in others unless the Board supplies a reasoned, evidence‑based justification for a different view. That principle preserves the integrity of the multifactor DuPont inquiry and reduces the opportunity for strategic narrowing that distorts the crowded‑field analysis. Practitioners should take this ruling as a prompt to litigate and document the universe of related goods and services comprehensively from the outset — and to press the Board for reasoned explanations whenever it deviates from its own prior factual framework.



# What Counts as “Credit Card Services” When the ID Manual Moves On?

## Why this case matters
Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a useful reminder that legacy identifications of services can have powerful—and sometimes surprising—reach long after the Trademark ID Manual removes an entry. The Federal Circuit affirmed the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board’s finding that “credit card services” overlapped significantly with “banking and financing services,” but it also corrected the Board for an inconsistent, and too-narrow, treatment of third‑party uses when measuring mark strength. The decision underscores two enduring points for trademark practitioners: tribunals will interpret legacy IDs using ordinary‑tool evidence (dictionaries, industry usage, third‑party registrations), and those interpretations can make services “legally identical, in part” even where the parties perform different roles in the payments ecosystem. How parties draft applications, prosecute registrations, and litigate oppositions should follow.

## What the court did
The Board had sustained CC Serve’s opposition to Apex’s intent‑to‑use registration for ASPIRE BANK, finding the marks likely to cause confusion. The Board read an older ID—“credit card services”—broadly to include issuance, processing, authorization, account management, monitoring and alerts, and counseling about card debt. Because Apex’s claimed “banking and financing services” overlap with those functions, the Board concluded the services were highly similar (indeed, “legally identical, in part”), and the second DuPont factor therefore weighed heavily in favor of confusion.

On appeal the Federal Circuit affirmed that holding: substantial evidence supported the Board’s conclusion that the services were highly similar. But the court vacated the Board’s analysis of the sixth DuPont factor (number and nature of similar marks in use) and, because the first‑factor (similarity of the marks) analysis depends on the mark’s commercial strength, vacated that too. The problem was procedural and conceptual: having found the services highly similar for DuPont two, the Board then restricted the universe of third‑party “Aspire” uses it considered to only those expressly labeled “credit card services,” excluding uses tied to broader banking and financing services. The Federal Circuit held that was inconsistent—once the Board adopts a scope of similarity for the services, it must keep that scope when addressing other relevant DuPont factors. The case was remanded for reconsideration of factors one and six in light of the Board’s findings on factor two.

## How tribunals interpret legacy identifications
A few practical lessons about method:

- Tribunals do not treat deletion from the ID Manual as an eraser. When a registration identifies services using an entry that the Office later deleted, the Board will still construe the words of the registration using ordinary tools: dictionary definitions, industry practice, and third‑party registrations and marketplace evidence.
- Dictionaries and definitional materials remain a common starting point. The Board here relied in part on conventional definitions for “banking,” “bank,” and “finance” to show overlap with functions typically thought of as “credit card services.”
- Third‑party registrations and real‑world evidence (how businesses actually brand and market services) matter for showing that different services can emanate from a single source and for evaluating commercial strength.
- The DuPont factors must be applied coherently. A Board finding that two services are highly similar should inform, not contradict, the analysis of third‑party use and mark strength under DuPont six and the overall commercial impression under DuPont one.

## The risk of expansive readings
Reading an older, short identification expansively can be decisive. A registrant with a relatively old or broadly worded registration can defeat an ITU applicant who intends to offer different—but functionally related—services. That can be especially consequential in the financial services and payments space, where banks, card issuers, processors, program managers, and servicers occupy overlapping but distinct roles. The Board’s definition here turned a business that partners with banks to run branded card programs into a competitor, for likelihood‑of‑confusion purposes, of a bank that does not issue cards—simply because the generic functions (issuing, processing, account servicing) sit within the same conceptual sphere.

But expansive readings carry risks too. Overbroad constructions can improperly magnify a registrant’s protection absent strong evidence of marketplace use or consumer perception; they can also invite appellate correction if the Board applies inconsistent scopes across DuPont factors, as happened here.

## Practical takeaways for applicants and registrants

For applicants (and their counsel):
- Don’t file broad ITU identifications unless you mean them. Where possible, tailor identifications to the specific services you will offer and to the channels and customers you will target (e.g., “retail banking services provided through an online platform” rather than just “banking services”).
- If you need broader coverage, build an evidentiary record to support non‑overlap: declarations about intended channels, sample marketing plans, and (for later proceedings) evidence showing distinct purchasers and trade channels.
- Consider limiting language by amendment or accepting a more specific identification during prosecution to reduce the risk of an opposition grounded on legacy definitions.
- If you cannot narrow at the PTO stage, be prepared to litigate on the factual record: show differences in function, purchasers, or ordinary trade channels; employ expert testimony or consumer surveys if appropriate.

For registrants (and their counsel):
- Preserve and develop evidence of actual use and licensing, particularly where the registration predates the ID Manual change. Specimens, partner agreements, and examples of how the mark is presented in the marketplace will support a broader reading.
- Use third‑party registrations and evidence to show the ordinary trade practice of bundling similar financial functions under single brands.
- When asserting likelihood of confusion, be consistent: if you rely on a broad reading of your registration to show overlap for DuPont two, make sure your inventory of third‑party uses for DuPont six reflects that same scope.

For both sides in litigation:
- Don’t treat the ID Manual as determinative. The Board and courts will interpret the words of the registration in light of contextual evidence.
- Be mindful that the Board must be consistent across DuPont factors. If a party gets a favorable factual finding on similarity, it should not be disadvantaged when the same factual scope is relevant to third‑party use and mark strength.

## Doctrinal implications and downstream effects
This decision reinforces that: (1) legacy identifications can be broadly consequential; (2) the Federal Circuit will police internal consistency in the Board’s DuPont analyses; and (3) proof of marketplace realities—how services actually operate and are presented—can be decisive. The remand on factors one and six opens the door for the Board to revisit the mark‑strength analysis: if the Board on remand considers the broader set of third‑party “Aspire” uses (including those tied to banking and financing services), it might find the mark weaker and narrow CC Serve’s protection, which could alter the overall likelihood‑of‑confusion calculus.

More broadly, expect practitioners in banking, payments, and adjacent sectors to pay greater attention to legacy IDs. Oppositions to ITU filings for digital or internet‑only banks (a growing trend) may increasingly invoke older registrations that courts and the Board will construe according to ordinary meaning, not the present contents of the ID Manual.

## Bottom line
Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a cautionary tale: words in a registration—old or new—can travel far. Deleting an entry from the ID Manual does not eliminate the need to define and, where necessary, defend or narrow the scope of services in a registration. The decision also fortifies a simple procedural rule with substantive bite: once the Board adopts a factual scope for service similarity, that scope must govern related DuPont inquiries. Practitioners should treat identifications as strategic drafting choices and should build evidentiary records on marketplace use and customer perception from the outset.



# Juice Generation, Recharged: Third-Party Use Evidence in a Crowded Financial Field

## Why Apex Bank v. CC Serve matters

The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a timely corrective to a recurring practical and doctrinal problem at the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board: when the Board finds two services to be highly similar (or legally identical in part), it cannot then narrow the universe of third‑party marks considered under DuPont factor six to a tighter slice of the market. In doing so, the court reaffirmed that evidence of third‑party registrations and use must be evaluated across the range of similar goods or services—not artificially constrained to a single subcategory—when gauging a mark’s commercial strength. For parties in the financial‑services space (and for TTAB practice generally), Apex Bank signals that a “crowded field” argument must be built and rebutted on the full marketplace the parties occupy.

## The basics of the case

CC Serve owns a long‑registered ASPIRE mark used in connection with credit‑card services; Apex sought to register ASPIRE BANK for banking and financing services (it does not itself issue credit cards). The TTAB sustained CC Serve’s opposition under Section 2(d), finding a high degree of overlap between “credit card services” and “banking and financing services” and concluding the ASPIRE mark was entitled to the normal scope of protection. Apex appealed, arguing among other things that the Board improperly narrowed its sixth DuPont analysis to marks used only in connection with credit‑card services, excluding “Aspire” uses across broader banking and financing services.

The Federal Circuit affirmed the Board’s finding that the parties’ services are highly similar, but it vacated and remanded the Board’s treatment of DuPont factor six (number and nature of similar marks in use) because the Board had limited its inquiry to credit‑card marks even after concluding the services were highly similar more broadly. Because factor one (the marks’ overall commercial impression) is linked to commercial strength, the court also vacated and remanded that factor for reconsideration in light of the expanded sixth‑factor analysis.

## How this fits with Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin

Apex Bank rides squarely on the reasoning in cases like Juice Generation and Jack Wolfskin: third‑party use and registration evidence can show that a field is “crowded,” reducing a mark’s commercial strength and narrowing the scope of protection. Juice Generation treated 26 third‑party marks as “a considerable number” indicating crowding, while Jack Wolfskin referred to “voluminous evidence” of similar third‑party uses. But the Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex reinforces an important corollary: the relevant universe of third‑party marks to assess for crowding must be defined consistently with the Board’s finding about the similarity of the parties’ services. If the Board finds services overlap substantially, it cannot then cabin the third‑party inquiry to a narrower subdomain on the premise that only that slice is “relevant.”

That principle prevents the Board from effectively nullifying the sixth factor by narrowing the comparator set after using a broader definition to find similarity on factor two. The court’s instruction avoids circular or cherry‑picked analyses: once you conclude the services are similar, you must consider third‑party uses across that same similarity scope.

## What third‑party evidence does (and doesn’t) prove

Two frequently conflated concepts arise in factor six disputes: third‑party registrations and third‑party use in commerce. Both can be relevant, but they carry different weight.

- Third‑party registrations can suggest that other applicants consider the term available and that the term serves a common or weak source‑identifying function. Registrations alone, however, are not proof that consumers have encountered the marks in the marketplace or been trained to distinguish among them.

- Actual marketplace use (websites, advertising, product or service offerings, merchant materials, press coverage, licensing or partnership agreements, consumer exposure metrics, sales and longevity data) is stronger evidence that consumers are familiar with the term in a competitive sense and are equipped to differentiate similar marks.

The Federal Circuit’s cited precedents treated both registration and use evidence as relevant, but they focused on the degree of marketplace appearance: courts have found 26 active third‑party marks persuasive, and Jack Wolfskin emphasized “voluminous” use. Apex emphasizes that the Board must look beyond a trimmed set of sub‑category uses when services overlap.

## Practical takeaways for practitioners in financial services

For counsel preparing opposition or defense records in the financial sector, Apex Bank suggests a few specific, practical moves.

For challengers seeking to show crowding (opposers or registrants attacking a mark’s strength):
- Do not limit searches or evidence to a single transactional label (e.g., “credit cards”) if the opponent’s services are alleged to overlap with broader banking or financing services. Gather examples across the full range of related financial services the Board has found similar.
- Prioritize documented marketplace use over bare registrations. Capture website screenshots, advertising, merchant program pages, product descriptions, press releases, and partnership announcements, and use the Wayback Machine or dated captures to establish longevity.
- Quantify exposure where possible: geographic reach, dates of first use, estimated user or account counts, number of issuing banks or partners, and (if available) media impressions or ad spend.
- Authenticate evidence with declarants who can attest to the sourcing and accuracy of copies, and where appropriate, obtain declarations from industry participants about common practice and consumer channels (e.g., how consumers access banking vs. card services).

For applicants/registrants seeking to defend strength:
- If the field is crowded, show why the particular mark has distinctiveness or secondary meaning: consumer surveys, brand recognition evidence, unique stylization, or substantial and exclusive use in a particular channel can help.
- Distinguish the relevant consumer: demonstrate differences in channels of trade, customer sophistication, or purchasing process—for instance, if a bank’s deposit‑account customers differ materially from credit‑card program customers, make that record explicit.
- Attack the quality of third‑party evidence: show many registrations lack proof of use, are geographically limited, brand names are descriptive/weak, or that the third parties operate in separate channels not likely to be encountered by the same consumer.

Do not rely on registrations alone. The Board and court will look to whether consumers actually confront the marks in commerce—especially where the contested mark is common or descriptive.

## Doctrinal and practical downstream effects

Apex Bank should nudge TTAB practice toward more disciplined, consistent DuPont analyses. Practically, expect:

- Broader third‑party searches and more voluminous records in oppositions and applications involving general terms in financial services. Parties will marshal broader evidence earlier in proceedings.
- More contested facts about the scope of similarity between services at the outset, since that finding materially shapes the scope of what third‑party evidence the Board must weigh.
- Increased reliance on authenticated marketplace evidence (screenshots, dated captures, declarations) and on metrics that show consumer exposure.
- More remands where the Board narrows the sixth factor after adopting a broader scope on the second factor; the Federal Circuit has now made that an error to watch for.

Finally, the decision reiterates a basic substantive point: trademark adjudicators must be consistent in defining the relevant market across DuPont factors. Narrowing the third‑party comparator set after acknowledging broader service overlap is legally untenable and strategically risky.

## Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve is a useful reminder that proof of a crowded field must be considered against the full backdrop of similarity the Board has already found. In financial services—where product lines and distribution channels often bleed into one another—counsel should think expansively when compiling third‑party evidence, prioritize demonstrable marketplace use, and be ready to defend or attack commercial‑strength arguments with concrete metrics that show whether consumers have in fact been trained to distinguish among similar marks. The Federal Circuit’s remand clears the way for the TTAB to take that broader view.



# ASPIRE vs. ASPIRE BANK: Strength as a Lens on Commercial Impression

## Why this case matters

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. turns on a lesson trademark practitioners have long known but sometimes forget: commercial strength shapes commercial impression. The Federal Circuit’s September 25, 2025 decision does not finally resolve whether ASPIRE BANK may register, but it makes clear that the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board must reassess the first DuPont factor (the marks’ overall commercial impression) after properly evaluating the sixth DuPont factor (the number and nature of similar marks in use). In practical terms, evidence that “ASPIRE” is common across the financial-services landscape could dilute CC Serve’s standard-character ASPIRE mark and change the likelihood-of-confusion calculus against Apex’s ASPIRE BANK filing. The remand therefore promises a careful, and potentially different, look at how market crowding influences whether consumers will mistake ASPIRE and ASPIRE BANK as coming from the same source.

## The Federal Circuit’s reasoning, in brief

The Board initially found the parties’ services highly similar—indeed, “legally identical, in part”—and held the second DuPont factor strongly in CC Serve’s favor. But when the Board turned to the sixth factor, it limited its inquiry to third-party uses of ASPIRE in connection with credit-card services only, excluding ASPIRE uses in broader banking and financing services. The Federal Circuit rejected that narrowing. Because the Board had already concluded the services were highly similar, the panel said, it must use that same scope when assessing crowdedness under the sixth factor: similar goods/services, not identical labels, govern what third-party marks are “similar.” Having confined the sixth-factor universe too narrowly, the Board’s conclusion that ASPIRE deserved the “normal” scope of protection could not stand. And because commercial impression under the first DuPont factor can be materially affected by how strong or weak a mark is in the marketplace, the court vacated the Board’s first-factor analysis as well and remanded both issues for reconsideration.

The court affirmed the Board only on the second DuPont factor—i.e., the services’ similarity—finding substantial evidence supported the Board’s determination.

## Strength as the pivot of commercial impression

This opinion is a tidy application of established principles: third-party usage that is “voluminous” or otherwise shows a crowded field can demonstrate conceptual or commercial weakness and narrow a mark’s scope of protection; a weaker mark will generate a different commercial impression than an inherently strong one. The Federal Circuit’s remand underscores two doctrinal points worth underscoring.

First, consistency in scope matters. Once the Board defines the relevant goods or services for the second DuPont factor, it cannot pick a different, narrower scope for the sixth factor without explaining why. The same marketplace that makes credit-card and banking/financing services “similar” for confusion purposes determines which competitors’ marks are relevant for assessing strength.

Second, the evaluation of strength is inherently comparative and factual. The Board cited only nine third‑party ASPIRE-formative marks in the credit-card space and concluded that was not “considerable” enough to show weakness. Apex claimed, and submitted evidence of, many more ASPIRE-formative uses across financial services; the Federal Circuit’s remand requires the Board to address that broader record. The upshot: a handful of third-party uses won’t suffice when the record suggests many more; conversely, even many registrations may be less probative than proof of real marketplace use and consumer recognition.

## How “BANK” and design elements will be treated on remand

The opinion itself does not rule on whether “BANK” will be treated as descriptive or whether Apex’s design elements will change the commercial impression. But familiar doctrinal rules give a preview of the issues the Board will confront.

“Bank” is a generic or highly descriptive term for banking services. In cases where an applicant’s mark pairs a potentially distinctive word with a generic or descriptive term (e.g., ASPIRE + BANK), tribunals often view the generic/descriptive element as weak or incapable of serving as the dominant source-identifier. That means the consumer is likelier to focus on “ASPIRE” as the distinctive portion of Apex’s mark. For a standard-character registration like CC Serve’s ASPIRE, protection extends across typefaces and stylizations, so word-only rights can be potent even where an applicant has a word-plus-design application. On the other hand, if the Board finds ASPIRE to be conceptually or commercially weak because of widespread third-party use across banking/financial services, the added, descriptive “BANK” and any modest design flourishes will do little to persuade a finding of likely confusion.

Thus on remand the Board must do at least three things: (1) consider the full universe of ASPIRE-formative marks used in banking and broader financial services consistent with its second-factor finding; (2) assess not just registrations but the nature and extent of actual marketplace use—how the public encounters the marks, length and geographic scope of use, and evidence of consumer perception or marketplace recognition; and (3) integrate that strength assessment into a fresh analysis of the overall commercial impression of ASPIRE versus ASPIRE BANK (and any design), keeping in mind that descriptive elements like “BANK” typically get little weight.

## What to watch for on remand

Practitioners should watch several discrete issues when the Board revisits the record. First, will the Board broaden the set of third-party marks it considers to include banking and financing uses (not just credit-card program marks)? The Federal Circuit expressly signaled that it should. Second, how will the Board treat third-party registrations versus documented uses? Registrations are informative but not conclusive; evidence that customers actually encounter many ASPIRE-brand financial services is far more persuasive of weakness. Third, will the Board give controlling weight to evidence showing a small number of uses (as it initially did) or to Apex’s contention of more pervasive use across financial subfields? And finally, will the Board explicitly analyze whether “BANK” functions as a merely descriptive element that fails to distinguish Apex’s mark from CC Serve’s word-only ASPIRE?

Each of these factual findings—how many relevant third-party uses truly exist, how consumers experience the marks, and the communicative force of “BANK” and any design—can tip the balance. If ASPIRE is shown to be commonplace in the financial services field, CC Serve’s mark will be entitled to only a narrow scope of protection and Apex’s ASPIRE BANK mark may survive. If ASPIRE remains commercially strong, or the Board finds that the third‑party uses are not sufficiently similar or pervasive, the refusal to register could be sustained.

## Doctrinal and practical implications

A few broader lessons follow from the Federal Circuit’s remand. Boards and practitioners must maintain doctrinal coherence across DuPont factors: findings about the similarity of services constrain the universe of relevant third‑party marks. Equally, applicants should be mindful of choosing marks built around common formative words in their industry; a crowded field invites an argument for a narrowed scope of protection. Registrants relying on long-standing standard-character registrations should document not only priority but also marketplace recognition and exclusivity of use where possible. Finally, the decision reiterates the practical importance of submitting real-world evidence—screen shots, advertising, sales, or consumer surveys—when arguing commercial strength or weakness.

## Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve is not a dramatic departure from settled DuPont doctrine, but it is an important reminder that strength is the lens through which commercial impression must be viewed. By insisting that the Board keep its scope consistent across factors, the Federal Circuit has ensured the likelihood-of-confusion inquiry will meaningfully account for market crowdedness. The remand will be the place to watch whether ASPIRE is a household financial-services signpost or one of many similar beacons in the banking and credit-card firmament—and that factual determination may well decide whether ASPIRE BANK can enter the register.



# Banks, fintechs, and the blurring lines of “banking and financing services”

## Why this case matters
The Federal Circuit’s decision in Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a practical wake-up call for brand teams at banks, fintech program managers, and lawyers who clear marks in the financial-services space. The court affirmed the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board’s finding that “banking and financing services” can overlap substantially with a provider’s “credit card services” even when the prior user (CC Serve) does not itself issue cards but operates credit‑card programs in partnership with issuing banks. At the same time, the court pushed back against a narrower Board approach to assessing third‑party use that would have limited consideration of similar marks to a narrowly defined subset of services. Taken together, the decision signals two things: (1) label‑based distinctions between “banking” and specialized payments or program‑management services will not always avoid confusion, and (2) the TTAB must consider the broader marketplace picture when measuring how crowded or weak a mark is.

## What the Federal Circuit did
The court affirmed in part, vacated in part, and remanded. It upheld the Board’s application of the second DuPont factor (similarity of goods/services): substantial evidence supported the Board’s conclusion that credit‑card services and Apex’s identified “banking and financing services” were highly similar and even “partially legally identical,” because the ordinary meanings of “banking” and “finance” include extending credit and activities tied to credit cards. But the court vacated the Board’s treatment of the sixth DuPont factor (number and nature of similar marks) and the first DuPont factor (similarity of the marks in appearance, sound, connotation and commercial impression). The reason: once the Board had found the parties’ services to be highly similar, it was inconsistent and legally improper to narrow the universe of third‑party marks considered under factor six to only those tied strictly to “credit card services.” The Federal Circuit reiterated that the sixth factor looks to marks in use on “similar” — not strictly identical — goods or services, and remanded for reconsideration of both the sixth and the first factors in light of the Board’s finding of high service overlap.

## Practical consequences for fintech program managers and bank partners
First, brand clearance in financial services now has to be broader. If your business is described in an application as “banking and financing services,” opponents will be able to point to prior uses and registrations across a range of credit and lending products, including credit‑card program managers and servicers that partner with issuing banks. Even if a prior user does not itself issue cards, its role in card program management, account servicing, authorization and processing is the kind of activity that the Board and court viewed as overlapping with “banking and financing.”

Second, do not assume contractual or structural distinctions (e.g., “we are only the program manager; the bank issues the card”) will immunize a mark from confusion. The Board’s and court’s focus was on how consumers experience services and on the ordinary scope of the terms used in identifications. Where consumers reasonably associate the services with a single source, that observation can drive a finding of similarity even when back‑end arrangements are complex.

Third, clearance searches and opposition strategies must look sideways across the financial services ecosystem. When evaluating third‑party use to show that a mark is weak or that confusion is unlikely, parties and examiners should consider marks used across related financial services — not just a hyper‑narrow subcategory. Similarly, an applicant facing an opposition can make its case more effectively by developing record evidence that the marketplace treats the services as distinct (different channels/consumers/pricing/marketing), or by marshaling third‑party registrations that show coexistence across the broader financial-services space.

## Positioning marks and practical clearance strategies
Several tactical takeaways follow for applicants and counsel:

- Draft identifications with care. Broad, high‑level identifications like “banking and financing services” invite overlap arguments. Narrowing identifications to specific services and channels (and being careful not to claim more than you actually provide) can limit exposure, although overly narrow identifications may reduce flexibility in use.

- Build record evidence of separation. If your model relies on program management or white‑label relationships, document and, if appropriate, submit declarations showing distinct trade channels, different consumer classes, separate branding on consumer‑facing materials, price differentials, or contractual limitations on use. Evidence that consumers do not associate the services with the same source can cut against a finding of similarity.

- Use coexistence/consent agreements proactively. Where a prior right exists, negotiated coexistence or consent agreements (and clear, visible co‑branding or disclaimers) can avoid litigation or TTAB opposition. These are especially valuable in crowded financial categories.

- Gather third‑party evidence yourself. An applicant seeking registration can submit third‑party registrations and marketplace examples showing that the term-at-issue is widely used across financial services, which may support narrower protection. Conversely, an opposer should assemble a broad set of third‑party uses across related financial services to show the mark’s weakness.

- Consider design and stylization. If the word element is weak or shared across the industry, distinct stylization, logos, or house marks tied to a longer history of use may strengthen protection. But expect the first DuPont factor (overall commercial impression) to be reanalyzed if the Board revisits commercial strength on remand, as the Federal Circuit instructed.

## Doctrinal takeaways and how TTAB will likely respond
The opinion reinforces two doctrinal points the Board and practitioners should internalize. First, similarity under DuPont is contextual: a finding of high overlap in one factor should inform consistent treatment under others. The Federal Circuit emphasized that when the Board finds services “highly similar,” it cannot then narrow the lens for third‑party evidence to a different, more restrictive subset. Second, the sixth DuPont factor asks about similar, not identical, goods and services; the Board must consider third‑party use across the relevant scope of similarity established in the record.

On remand the TTAB will likely reexamine the universe of third‑party “Aspire” uses across banking, lending, card issuance and program management. If the Board finds a larger body of prior use, it could conclude the ASPIRE mark is relatively weak and narrow its protection, which in turn could change the first factor analysis and the ultimate likelihood‑of‑confusion calculus. If it finds only a modest set of third‑party uses, the original likelihood‑of‑confusion finding may stand.

Expect practitioners to adapt. Oppositions and examiners will press for broader (cross‑category) searches and records that establish how consumers perceive financial services. Applicants will increasingly rely on marketplace evidence, narrower identifications, or negotiated coexistence to thread the needle between branding flexibility and registrability.

## Bottom line
Apex Bank v. CC Serve underscores that in modern financial services the line between “banking” and adjacent card/payment/program services is porous for trademark purposes. Brand owners can’t hide behind corporate roles or contractual labels; the TTAB and the courts look to how services are described, marketed, and perceived. For fintech program managers and bank partners, the practical response is straightforward: broaden clearance efforts, craft identifications and records with care, and be ready to show (or contest) how consumers experience the services across the financial ecosystem. The Federal Circuit’s remand on the sixth and first DuPont factors means that questions about how crowded the field is and how consumers actually perceive ASPIRE‑style marks will still be litigated — and the outcome will matter for how widely a financial‑services mark can be enforced.



# Standards of Review Matter: Parsing Legal Error from Substantial Evidence in TTAB Appeals

## Why this case matters

Apex Bank v. CC Serve Corp. is a tidy reminder of a basic appellate truth in trademark oppositions: whether an error is reversible often turns on whether it was one of law or one of fact. The Federal Circuit affirmed the Trademark Trial and Appeal Board’s factual finding that the parties’ services are highly similar under the second DuPont factor but nonetheless vacated the Board’s analysis of the sixth DuPont factor (and, because of its interdependence, the first factor) for legal error. The decision shows how a legal misstep—here, an inconsistent narrowing of the universe of third‑party marks—can contaminate the Board’s reasoning even where other findings enjoy substantial‑evidence support. For practitioners, the opinion is a useful lesson in appellate focus: preserve precise legal objections to the Board’s methodology, and build the record and argumentation to make those legal errors readily reviewable.

## What the court did

The Federal Circuit applied the familiar bifurcated standard of review: legal conclusions de novo, factual findings for substantial evidence. In this case the Board found that Apex’s proposed ASPIRE BANK mark and CC Serve’s earlier ASPIRE registration covered highly similar services—indeed, the Board concluded the services were “legally identical, in part” under the second DuPont factor, and the Federal Circuit affirmed that factual conclusion as supported by substantial evidence.

Where the Board ran into trouble was the sixth DuPont factor, which asks whether there are numerous third‑party uses of similar marks on similar goods or services (evidence that can show a mark is weak and entitled to a narrower scope of protection). Having already concluded the parties’ services were highly similar, the Board nevertheless restricted its sixth‑factor inquiry to third‑party uses tied narrowly to “credit card services,” excluding third‑party ASPIRE uses in other banking and financing contexts. The Federal Circuit held that restriction was legally erroneous because the sixth factor asks about similar—not identical—goods and services, and the Board’s own second‑factor finding required it to consider a broader set of third‑party uses. Because the sixth factor bears directly on the mark’s commercial strength—and commercial strength in turn figures heavily in the first‑factor comparison of overall commercial impression—the Court vacated the Board’s sixth‑factor and first‑factor findings and remanded for reconsideration consistent with the correct legal standard.

## Why the legal/factual distinction matters here

Two principles are on display. First, substantial‑evidence review is deferential. If the Board’s factual conclusions have record support, an appellate court will generally leave them undisturbed. That explains why the Federal Circuit affirmed the second DuPont factor: the Board examined the service descriptions, relied on dictionary definitions and third‑party registrations, and reached a reasonable factual conclusion about overlap.

Second, legal errors are reversible. The scope-of-similarity test the Board applied under the sixth factor was legal in nature—essentially a misconstruction of the relevant legal standard—and the Federal Circuit corrected it de novo. Where the Board applied a stricter standard for similarity under the sixth factor than it had for the second, the Court treated that as a legal inconsistency that could not be glossed over simply because other findings were supported by evidence.

The opinion thus underscores that an appellate court will not simply sanitize an internally inconsistent administrative analysis by picking and choosing findings to uphold. When a legal error infects the analytical framework—particularly in areas where factors are logically interlinked—it can force recalibration of other parts of the decision.

## Doctrinal implications and downstream effects

Apex Bank cements some pragmatic rules about DuPont practice. Most important is the expectation of coherence across factors: once the Board establishes a scope of similarity between services for one factor, it should not narrow its lens under related factors in a way that defeats that earlier finding. The sixth factor asks about “similar goods” and need not be limited to identical ones; a Board that treats it as requiring identity risks reversal.

The decision also reiterates the continuing importance of third‑party use evidence in assessing commercial strength. But it reminds practitioners that the persuasive force of such evidence depends on how broadly the Board defines the relevant field. A Board’s artificial narrowing of the reference class for third‑party marks can undercount relevant uses and overstate a registrant’s strength.

Finally, the case highlights that the first DuPont factor—overall commercial impression—is not insulated from error in a companion factor. When commercial strength is put in doubt by a legally flawed sixth‑factor analysis, the first factor must be revisited, because commercial impression is informed by strength of the mark.

## Practical takeaways for litigants and counsel

For TTAB litigants and their appellate counsel, the decision translates into several concrete strategies. First, preserve specific objections to legal standards and definitions the Board uses—especially the definition of the relevant public and the scope of “similar goods or services.” A clear record showing that the Board applied an inconsistent or too‑narrow legal standard will be the fastest route to reversal on legal grounds.

Second, when building the sixth‑factor record, submit third‑party registrations and use evidence framed broadly across the relevant field of services, not just the narrow service label the registrant used. If the parties’ services overlap in the second‑factor analysis, make sure the Board does not cabin the sixth factor to a narrower conception that undermines the connection.

Third, appreciate the uphill nature of substantial‑evidence challenges. If you can show error of law—misstatement of the governing legal test, inconsistent scope across factors, or an impermissible requirement of identity—you stand a far better chance than by arguing that the Board’s factfinding was just wrong. The Federal Circuit’s deference on facts means that appellate strategy should foreground legal arguments.

Finally, anticipate remediation on remand: if the Board is ordered to re‑examine the sixth factor under an appropriately broad conception of similar services, expect it to re‑weigh the mark’s strength and then to revisit first‑factor comparisons and any downstream DuPont balancing that depends on strength. Parties should be prepared to supply supplemental evidence of third‑party use and consumer perception, and to brief clearly how those materials bear on commercial strength and confusion likelihood.

## Conclusion

Apex Bank v. CC Serve is not novel as doctrinal law, but it is pedagogically useful. It shows the different bite that legal error and factual insufficiency have on appeal, reminds practitioners that DuPont factors are interdependent, and reinforces the importance of consistent scope across the Board’s analyses. For lawyers, the practical lesson is straightforward: build the record broadly where similarity is contested, preserve precise legal challenges to the Board’s standards, and aim your appellate brief at legal errors that will survive de novo review rather than relying solely on an argument that the Board got the facts wrong.